<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hummay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1075–1086</span> · Also known as Humai, Hume</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hummay was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> in the late eleventh century and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a>, which ruled Kanem and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> until the nineteenth century. Kanem tradition, including a charter of privileges known as his mahram, holds that he adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, making him the first Muslim ruler of the kingdom. His accession ended the rule of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duguwa-dynasty">Duguwa dynasty</a>.</p></div>
